WEBVTT


00:00:00.305 --> 00:00:07.305
Hello, welcome back to our course, and today we are going to continue talking about programming in R.

00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:15.221
So let us talk about apply function families. So this is a set of highly optimized functions for iterative computations,

00:00:15.110 --> 00:00:26.110
and it's much faster than standard loops. And, there are various versions for matrices, vectors, lists, etc.

00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:31.252
So we have an apply function. So this is a function used on matrices.

00:00:31.281 --> 00:00:35.281
We have lapply, this is used on lists and vectors,

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:42.869
it returns a list, we have sapply, like lapply, but returns a vector or a matrix.

00:00:53.181 --> 00:01:00.181
mapply, this is a multivariate version of sapply, and tapply, so this is like, applied version with a table grouped by factors. Alright, so let us start from apply functions. So apply function

00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:11.343
(it) applies a function sequentially to the rows, columns of a matrix, and it returns a vector. Okay, let us look at an example. I define a matrix A.

00:01:12.134 --> 00:01:15.134
You can see this is a matrix over with two rows.

00:01:15.221 --> 00:01:20.221
And then I would like to apply this function by row. 

00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:27.563
What am I doing? I pass a matrix A. I would like to apply means

00:01:27.066 --> 00:01:34.066
to each row of my matrix. So, mean for the first row, 

00:01:34.843 --> 00:01:37.843
mean for the second row. And indeed, I get two numbers.

00:01:45.986 --> 00:01:48.986
If I would like to apply my function mean by columns. Then I select margin to be 2, and indeed, you can see that I computed means for each column.

00:01:54.044 --> 00:01:59.044
lapply applies a function sequentially to a vector or a list. And it returns a list. So here

00:02:04.902 --> 00:02:06.902
we defined these two vectors. So these are realisations from normal distribution, and then,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.360
this C object is a list composed of these two vectors, A and B.

00:02:12.725 --> 00:02:17.725
And then we apply the min function to each element of the list C.

00:02:22.804 --> 00:02:27.804
So, if I now use lapply, applied to that list, and I select mean, then I compute a mean for the first element,

00:02:29.838 --> 00:02:33.838
so basically A, and the mean for the second element. B.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:43.660
sapply applies a function sequentially to a vector or a list, and it returns a vector or matrix.

00:02:43.965 --> 00:02:48.965
So here, let's just look at this example. We have two matrices,

00:02:53.904 --> 00:02:56.904
A and B, with two rows. And then, we bundled them together in that list.

00:03:01.845 --> 00:03:04.845
And then you can see that, I apply sapply to the list, and then

00:03:04.564 --> 00:03:09.564
the first function I would like to apply for this list is to return

00:03:09.456 --> 00:03:15.456
these matrices as vectors. So indeed, I have the first matrix as vector,

00:03:18.649 --> 00:03:22.649
and the second matrix as vector. Another way to kind of test what

00:03:22.777 --> 00:03:27.777
sapply does is to see what happens if I apply sapply to my list, 

00:03:27.575 --> 00:03:35.575
and then I ask it to compute mean. So you can see that it computes mean to each

00:03:41.988 --> 00:03:45.988
element of my list, so 4.5 and 3.5. Now, mapply function, so mapply is a multivariate version of sapply.

00:03:48.724 --> 00:03:53.724
It applies FUN function to the first, second, third elements of argument vectors.

00:03:59.438 --> 00:04:03.438
So here, I define these two vectors, A and B, with elements 1 and 2, and minus 1 and minus 2, and I would like to apply

00:04:04.116 --> 00:04:09.116
function mapply, and now this is my function, so basically I would like to

00:04:09.936 --> 00:04:12.936
add. What I would like to add? I would like to add

00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:26.851
each element of my vector. So basically, I'm adding the first element of vector A to the first element of vector B, I get 0. The second element of vector A, and the second element of vector B. I get 0. 

00:04:31.191 --> 00:04:35.191
Now, tapply applies a function to the elements of a vector

00:04:38.185 --> 00:04:40.185
grouped by factor. So let us access our IRIS data.

00:04:43.045 --> 00:04:45.045
And then apply function mean to sepal.width by species.

00:04:46.528 --> 00:04:52.528
So here, I apply a function tapply to my sepal.width,

00:04:57.603 --> 00:05:00.603
and then, here, by what? By different types of species of irises.

00:05:00.861 --> 00:05:06.861
And what function am I applying? I'm applying mean function, so you can see that, indeed, I have 3 different means

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:16.150
of separate widths with respect to different species. Okay, so oftentimes it's very useful to take time,

00:05:16.006 --> 00:05:21.006
to track how much time it takes to perform certain computations.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:27.489
And basically, system.time function allows us to do it. So here you can see that

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:34.840
I define this vector with zeros, and it's a long vector, and I would like to compute

00:05:34.597 --> 00:05:39.597
how much time it takes to fill this vector with

00:05:46.446 --> 00:05:48.446
realizations of random variable from a normal distribution step by step.

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.990
So basically, I created that for loop for i in 1, 2, ..., 50,000,

00:05:59.468 --> 00:06:01.468
and I generate these numbers one by one. And you can see the time which has elapsed.

00:06:07.053 --> 00:06:09.053
In contrast. If I do it in one shot, using pre-programmed function, you can see that the time

00:06:18.557 --> 00:06:21.557
is shorter, so it's often recommended to check if there exists, like, highly efficiently programmed function in R, because it saves us time.

00:06:23.904 --> 00:06:26.904
Especially if we need to repeat certain time-consuming chunks many times.

00:06:28.497 --> 00:06:36.497
And, indeed, how can we make our code faster? So, we can use functions of the apply family.

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:46.736
Instead of loops, whenever possible. If you use a loop, create the object, a vector matrix, in advance and fill it inside the loop.

00:06:50.623 --> 00:06:54.623
Never use cbind or rbind inside a loop. You could always parallelize

00:07:01.493 --> 00:07:05.493
to distribute CPU time to several cores. And code that can be expressed as a loop with independent iterations can be parallelized.

00:07:05.308 --> 00:07:13.308
And package parallel, it's pre-installed in R, and it can be used to basically redistribute

00:07:21.126 --> 00:07:24.126
certain tasks to certain CPUs. And, there are some helpful packages for profiling and micro-benchmarking.

00:07:31.343 --> 00:07:34.343
These are profvis, and bench. And finally, debugging. So when writing a more complicated code or functions

00:07:34.682 --> 00:07:38.682
with nested functions inside, it can be hard to find the part

00:07:41.171 --> 00:07:43.171
responsible for an error. And function trace back

00:07:51.671 --> 00:07:55.671
can be called after an error occurred, and then we can set certain options. So, for example, options error=recover, opens an interactive debugger

00:08:00.885 --> 00:08:03.885
whenever an error occurs. And when finished, set options back to default with options(error = NULL).

00:08:03.413 --> 00:08:11.413
Browser parenthesis opes an interactive debugger

00:08:19.318 --> 00:08:22.318
at the respective position in the source code. And then, debug, and debugonce inside the browser function in the first line

00:08:26.447 --> 00:08:30.447
Of the source code of the respective function. And, undebug removes it.

00:08:41.517 --> 00:08:44.517
So these are some options how to deal with debugging and finding bugs, but once again, it's great if you can test it, and then, you know, like, see what works for you.

00:08:49.591 --> 00:08:51.591
And a small example towards the end. So here you can see that I set these options to error.

00:08:51.299 --> 00:08:58.299
And to recover, and then I define two functions. First function is this function which returns

00:09:02.349 --> 00:09:04.349
logarithm of X. And the second function is a function which return a letter.

00:09:06.897 --> 00:09:12.897
And now, what happens? I apply first function G.

00:09:12.202 --> 00:09:18.202
And then I insert 1 to G, so it returns A for me.

00:09:18.951 --> 00:09:24.951
But then I apply F function, so logarithm of A. And indeed, here, I can see

00:09:28.347 --> 00:09:32.347
that I get an error, because a non-numeric argument was passed to a mathematical function.

00:09:32.798 --> 00:09:40.798
And then, once I set that option to recover, then you will see that window, basically in the console, you will be asked to

00:09:45.784 --> 00:09:49.784
enter a frame number, or 0 to exit. And then if you select one, then it opens debugging environment.

00:09:58.469 --> 00:10:00.469
and you can play with it. And then, after you are done with debugging, you should reset your options(error = NULL). Thank you!

